    {
      "title": "Shamonda virus detected in human for first time in France",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/22/shamonda-virus-detected-in-human-first-time-france",
      "description": "<p>Investigations under way to determine whether virus normally found in livestock contributed to man’s death</p><p>A man in France has become the first human known to have contracted Shamonda virus, with investigations under way to explore whether it contributed to his death.</p><p>Shamonda virus is a type of bunyavirus and is spread by bites from infected midges. According to the <a href=\"https://www.ages.at/en/human/disease/pathogens-from-a-to-z/shamonda-virus\">Austrian Agency for Health and Food Safety</a>, in animals such as cattle, sheep and goats it can cause diarrhoea, lack of appetite and a decline in milk yield, “as well as abortions and congenital abnormalities in newborn calves, lambs and kids”.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/22/shamonda-virus-detected-in-human-first-time-france\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-22T15:07:01"
    },
    {
      "title": "France reports first human Shamonda virus case, says no health risk",
      "link": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/22/france-reports-first-human-shamonda-virus-case-says-no-health-risk?traffic_source=rss",
      "description": "Until now, the virus was considered non-transferable to humans, making the patient the first documented human case.",
      "source_name": "Al Jazeera All News",
